## Subconscious memory (subconscious-mcp)

This project uses local semantic memory via the `subconscious-mcp` MCP server.
Memory stays on disk under `~/.subconscious-mcp/` and is already isolated
per-project (the namespace is derived from this repo).

### Before non-trivial work

Call `recall` with a one-sentence task description, then:

1. Hit at >= 0.88: reuse the answer after a quick sanity check.
2. Hit below 0.88: verify the answer fits this task before acting on it.
3. Miss with best similarity > 0.7: call `echo` first. If the nearest entry
   is a different task family, work fresh; only lower the threshold when echo
   shows the same family.
4. Miss at <= 0.7: do the work normally.

### After solving something reusable

Call `remember` with the task phrasing you would expect next time and a
concise answer (commands, paths, decisions). Tag with this repo name.
Do not remember secrets, API keys, one-off debugging, or stale file contents.

If `remember` returns `warning="near_duplicate"`, inspect `nearest_entry_id`
and decide: merge/update (forget the old one, re-remember combined) or proceed
if it is genuinely distinct.

### Hygiene

Run `drift_report` periodically. For flagged entries, `forget` and re-remember
more specific variants, or tighten the recall threshold for that family. Use
`stats` to check `hit_rate_last_100`. Never trust a hit above 0.85 on
interpretive tasks; verify first.
